DISCLOSURE

@sasjs/adapter is a client library for SAS servers, published by 4GL Apps
(https://sasapps.io). It is not a security tool, a penetration-testing
utility or an obfuscation tool. It does provide capabilities that
automated scanning can read as dual-use, so this file declares them and
their intended legitimate use.

Capabilities

- HTTP(S) requests to a SAS server. The destination is a base URL supplied
  by the host application's own configuration (sasjsconfig.json, a
  constructor argument, or an environment variable the application sets).
  The package contains no hard-coded destination and does not contact any
  host the host application has not been given.

- Credential and token handling. It sends credentials to that server as
  part of its authentication flow: username and password, an access token,
  or a Viya client id and secret for OAuth. Tokens are held in memory for
  the session. Base64 is used for HTTP Basic headers and for SAS content
  encoding, not for obfuscation.

- File transfer. It uploads local files to the SAS server, downloads
  results, and writes response streams and log files under the project
  directory.

- Job execution. On SAS 9 deployments it calls the server's job-execution
  endpoints. The Node build exposes the same API surface as the browser
  build.

Intended use

Applications that talk to a SAS server the operator owns or is authorised
to use: SASjs applications, the SASjs CLI, and Data Controller for SAS.
Every destination and every credential comes from the host application's
own configuration.

Packaging note

The published JavaScript is webpack output and bundles third-party
dependencies, so it contains constructs that can appear in a scan but are
not authored here, including webpack's globalThis polyfill and its module
runtime.

Source: https://github.com/sasjs/adapter
